Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Lab 2, model planetary motion", "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A much simpler approach is to fit the data directly to the equations (in a \
Cartesian coordinate system with the origin as the Earth):\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[{
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(x = \ 
          A\_x\ \(cos(\[Omega]\ t\  + \ \[Alpha]\_x)\);\)\), 
    "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(y = \ 
          A\_y\ \(cos(\[Omega]\ t\  + \[Alpha]\_y)\);\)\), 
    "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      \(TraditionalForm\`z = \ \(A\_z\) \(\(cos(\[Omega]\ t\  + \ \
\[Alpha]\_z)\)\(.\)\)\)}]],
  "\nHowever, the fitting is a nonlinear problem.  So to do it properly is \
difficult.  I'll be agreeable if you just `read' from the figures to get the \
parameters.  As you can see, in general, this will not be a circle as we \
don't have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\^2 + y\^2 + z\^2 = 1. \)]],
  "\n\nBelow is a much more sophiscated way of doing things.  Hope you can \
learn something from it.  Note that I have used linear algebra methods \
several times.   Before we can reading in the data in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", I removed the column contains * and m and A, and the date/time columns \
to use only the Julian date.   I also make the sign ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`+\)]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`-\)]],
  " in a separate column.  It is important to do this to treat the negative \
declination correctly.  I don't explain ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " features, assuming you know them. Now we read in the Moon data as \
follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(moonrawdata = 
        Import["\<D:\\WangJS\\teaching\\CZ3102\\moon.dat\>", \ \
"\<table\>"];\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The raw data are in the following format (first and last 2 lines)  [Julian \
date, RA hr, RA min, RA sec, sign, DEC deg, DEC min, DEC sec]:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Short[moonrawdata, 10]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[\({{2.45227516666667`*^6, 7, 57, 18.76`, "+", 23, 44, 
          1.`}, {2.45227533333333`*^6, 8, 3, 30.72`, "+", 23, 25, 
          21.5`}, \[LeftSkeleton]4605\[RightSkeleton], {2.453043`*^6, 10, 25, 
          30.5`, "+", 15, 21, 27.3`}, {2.45304316666667`*^6, 10, 31, 47.99`, 
          "+", 14, 42, 19.4`}}\),
      (Short[ #, 10]&)]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We convert them in more suitable format for computation,we subtract the \
Julian date value at 1 Jan 2002  at 00.00 (12 midnight) (2452275.16666667), \
so that time begin at 0 for that date and time.  We also use radian for the \
two angles.  First number is RA and second is DEC, both in radian, in terms \
of the angle 0<\[Phi]<2\[Pi], and  0< \[Theta] < \[Pi].  This is in standard \
spherical polar coordinate system.   Define sign[ ] to convert string ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(\*"\"\<\>"\)\(+\)\) \*"\"\<\>"\)]],
  " and \"",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`-\)]],
  "\" to the number 1 and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`-\)]],
  "1."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sign["\<+\>"] := 1;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(sign["\<-\>"] := \(-1\);\)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(General::"spell1" \(\(:\)\(\ \)\) 
      "Possible spelling error: new symbol name \"\!\(sign\)\" is similar to \
existing symbol \"\!\(Sign\)\"."\)], "Message"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(moondata\  = \ 
        Map[\ {\ #[\([1]\)] - 
                2452275.16666667, \ \((#[\([2]\)] + #[\([3]\)]/
                      60.0 + #[\([4]\)]/3600.0)\)*
                Pi/12.0, \ \((90.0\  - \ 
                    sign[#[\([5]\)]]*\((#[\([6]\)] + #[\([7]\)]/
                            60.0 + #[\([8]\)]/3600.0)\))\)*\ Pi/180.0} &, \ 
          moonrawdata];\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Short[moondata, 10]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[\({{0.`, 2.08266939870188`, 
          1.1565666695180978`}, {0.1666666604578495`, 2.109719093225706`, 
          1.1619941586781193`}, \[LeftSkeleton]4605\[RightSkeleton], \
{767.8333333302289`, 2.7292949788322156`, 1.302755932478824`}, {768.`, 
          2.7567468263045214`, 1.314138872897595`}}\),
      (Short[ #, 10]&)]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We will use the model I, geocentrical circular orbit.  It turns out the \
mathematics involved for other models are rather complicated.  First we need \
to determine the plane the orbit lays.  Note that a point on a plane (the \
equation for a plane) is\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`A\ x\  + \ B\ y\  + \ C\ z\  = \ 0\)]],
  "\nwhere ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\((A, B, C)\)\)]],
  " is the direction vector normal to the plane.  This direction can be \
determined by a least-quare method.  Each data point is a set ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\((x, y, z)\)\)]],
  ".   Thus we have an over-determined system of equations to solve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`A, \ B, \ C\)]],
  ".   The matrix is:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(planeMat\  = \ 
        Map[\ {Sin[#[\([3]\)]]*Cos[#[\([2]\)]], \ 
              Sin[#[\([3]\)]]*\ Sin[#[\([2]\)]], \ Cos[#[\([3]\)]]} &, \ 
          moondata];\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Short[planeMat, 10]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[\({{\(-0.44838613370670777`\), 0.7980951161874213`, 
          0.4024848576249009`}, {\(-0.4709218780561059`\), 
          0.7875392044266581`, 
          0.3975104857222465`}, \[LeftSkeleton]4605\[RightSkeleton], \
{\(-0.883486524569788`\), 0.3864066629523991`, 
          0.2648423148395821`}, {\(-0.8964961071721347`\), 
          0.3631190662698258`, 0.2538489187224133`}}\),
      (Short[ #, 10]&)]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The matrix has the property\n \t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"M", " ", 
            RowBox[{"(", "\[NegativeThinSpace]", GridBox[{
                  {\(A\[IndentingNewLine]
                    B\)},
                  {"C"}
                  }], "\[NegativeThinSpace]", ")"}]}], "=", "0"}], 
        TraditionalForm]]],
  "\nthus we can used pseudo-inverse to find equation for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`A, \ B, \ C\)]],
  ".  However, this is not correct.  Since  if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`A, \ B, \ C\)]],
  " is a solution, so is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\[Lambda]A, \ \[Lambda]B, \ \[Lambda]C\)]],
  " also a solution.  So what is important is only the direction of the \
plane.  We can fix this  problem by requiring a unit vector. Thus we have to \
solve the problem of minimising \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\[Sum]\_i\ \((A\ x\_\(\(i\)\(\ \)\) + \ B\ y\_i + C\ \
z\_\(\(i\)\(\ \)\))\)\^2\)]],
  "\nsubject to the constraint \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`A\^2 + B\^2 + C\^2 = 0. \)]],
  "\nUsing Lagrange multiplier method, this results to the following eigen \
value problem: \n \t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      FormBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{\(M\^T\), "M", " ", 
            RowBox[{"(", "\[NegativeThinSpace]", GridBox[{
                  {"A"},
                  {\(B\[IndentingNewLine]
                    C\)}
                  }], "\[NegativeThinSpace]", ")"}]}], " ", "=", 
          RowBox[{"\[Lambda]", "  \[NegativeThinSpace]\[NegativeThinSpace]", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"(", "\[NegativeThinSpace]", GridBox[{
                    {\(A\[IndentingNewLine]
                      B\)},
                    {"C"}
                    }], "\[NegativeThinSpace]", ")"}], "."}]}]}], 
        TraditionalForm]]],
  "\n  The solution is find using Eigensystem[ ]."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M2\  = \ Transpose[planeMat] . \ planeMat\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{2290.852302711043`, 
        75.2710583173066`, \(-168.6466706538081`\)}, {75.2710583173066`, 
        1878.6115678097422`, 888.487906790956`}, {\(-168.6466706538081`\), 
        888.487906790956`, 439.53612947921494`}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(res\  = \ Eigensystem[M2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{2308.999109666295`, 2299.116443003486`, 
        0.8844473302186608`}, {{0.815850159323016`, \
\(-0.49028284505292075`\), \(-0.30661253950127876`\)}, {0.5726521122082786`, 
          0.7586963998444773`, 
          0.3105629263875937`}, {\(-0.08036215474952267`\), 
          0.428955131348092`, \(-0.8997440854899547`\)}}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The three eigen vectors correspond to exactly the three directions of our \
new coordinates, the last one being our normal direction of the plane.  Why?  \
e1, e2, and e3 will be the unit vectors of three direction of the axis.  They \
are otho-normal.  R is precisely the needed rotation matrix to transform from \
the old coordinate system ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\((x, y, z)\)\)]],
  " into the new one ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\((x', y', z')\)\)]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({e1, e2, e3}\  = \ \(R = \ res[\([2]\)]\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{0.815850159323016`, \(-0.49028284505292075`\), \
\(-0.30661253950127876`\)}, {0.5726521122082786`, 0.7586963998444773`, 
        0.3105629263875937`}, {\(-0.08036215474952267`\), 
        0.428955131348092`, \(-0.8997440854899547`\)}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(newcoord\ \  = \ Map[R . # &, planeMat];\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(coordXY\  = \ Map[\ Take[#, 2] &, newcoord];\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Short[coordXY, 12]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[\({{\(-0.8805151471172404`\), 
          0.4737395000434898`}, {\(-0.8922003504834685`\), 
          0.4512807706966256`}, {\(-0.9072709739819128`\), 
          0.41984844045086933`}, {\(-0.9274220055324347`\), 
          0.3730078268008742`}, \[LeftSkeleton]4602\[RightSkeleton], \
{\(-0.9961449135582826`\), \(-0.08764943029505588`\)}, {\(-0.991445154610068`\
\), \(-0.13051487601669173`\)}, {\(-0.9872708023917128`\), \
\(-0.1590471980060581`\)}}\),
      (Short[ #, 12]&)]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "coordXY give pair of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\((x, y)\)\)]],
  " coordinates in the new system, note that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`z\)]],
  " is approximately zero.  We can make a plot of the orbit projected onto \
this plane.  We see the moon orbit is nearly a perfect circle. We plot the \
first 170 points only (about 1 revolution)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ListPlot[Take[coordXY, 170], \ AspectRatio\  \[Rule] \ 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[GraphicsData["PostScript", "\<\
