  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`y' \((t)\) = sin(\[Omega]\ t\  + \ \[Alpha])\)]],
  ".  This is a \"uniform circular motion\". To do this, we compute the angle \
by arctan(y/x) and fit it to a straight line.  We need to be careful when we \
do this as the inverse tangent function is multiple values, we need to choose \
the right branch.  To do this, we need to define a little funtion to remember \
the phase (wind is the winding number):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(angle[co_] := 
      Module[{wind, a, b, dat, l\  = \ Length[co], \ 
          i}, \[IndentingNewLine]wind\  = \ 
          0; \[IndentingNewLine]b = \ \(dat\  = \ {ArcTan[co[\([1, 1]\)], 
                co[\([1, 2]\)]]}\); \[IndentingNewLine]For[i\  = 2, 
          i \[LessEqual] \ 
            l, \(++i\), \[IndentingNewLine]a\  = \ \ ArcTan @@ \ 
              co[\([i]\)]; \[IndentingNewLine]If[
            b\  > \ 0.0\  && \ 
              a\  < \ 0.0, \ \(++wind\)]; \[IndentingNewLine]b\  = \ 
            a; \[IndentingNewLine]AppendTo[dat, \ 
            a\  + \ 2.0*Pi*
                wind];\[IndentingNewLine]]; \[IndentingNewLine]dat\
\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(angledata\  = \ angle[coordXY];\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Short[angledata, 30]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[\({2.64798946606115`, 2.67331061125764`, 2.7081785718685025`, 
        2.7591923748014042`, 2.8178543902343702`, 2.8692605719727626`, 
        2.9048625049914243`, 2.930769680705283`, 2.962859728507125`, 
        3.010639347318967`, 3.068584259365948`, 3.122498699225076`, 
        3.1617724060737884`, 3.188981564041765`, 3.218579800251607`, 
        3.2624691471562217`, 3.318531129294015`, 3.3740186387246762`, 
        3.416841710052193`, 3.4460016859187634`, 3.473626505450144`, 
        3.513290694732626`, \[LeftSkeleton]4566\[RightSkeleton], 
        178.50017057669712`, 178.53262909444052`, 178.55373888621662`, 
        178.57796331810695`, 178.6166764839584`, 178.6671429094552`, 
        178.71621844146293`, 178.7521489510529`, 178.77520634397834`, 
        178.79849675608395`, 178.83500944140042`, 178.884683790812`, 
        178.93565649950386`, 178.97519434643553`, 179.00085383937588`, 
        179.0237427562383`, 179.0579323611534`, 179.1062163662881`, 
        179.15854386836085`, 179.20166969083775`, 179.23050677410288`}\),
      (Short[ #, 30]&)]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is the angle data vs the point number (each point is 4 hours apart \
for moon, or 4/24=1/6 of a Julian date).  We combine the equally spaced time \
data (Julian date offset from 1 Jan 2002) with the angle data to make a \
straight line fit.  The result is  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(\[Alpha]\)\(=\)\)\)]],
  "2.69182\[InvisibleSpace]",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`+\)]],
  "0.229975 ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`t\)]],
  ", see below.  This little result is the final result for (x'(t),y'(t)).   \
Of course, z'(t)=0.   To get (x,y,z), we need to inverse transform ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`R\^\(-1\) = R\^T\)]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(juliavsangle\  = \ 
        Table[\ {\((j - 1)\)*1.0/6.0, \ angledata[\([j]\)]}, \ {j, \ 1, \ 
            Length[angledata]}]\ ;\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(alpha[t_] = Fit[juliavsangle, \ {1, \ t}, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\(2.6918195108837377`\)\(\[InvisibleSpace]\)\) + 
      0.22997486164277603`\ t\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The last (offset) Julian date 768.0 is 0:00 clock (midnight) of Sunday, 8 \
Feb 2004.  Thus 9:00pm of Monday, 9 Feb 2004 is  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`t = \(768.0 + 1.0\  + \ 
            21.0/24.0 = \(\(769.875\)\(.\)\)\)\)]],
  "  We define the function predict[t] to give the predicted value of RA and \
DEC.  We have invert from Cartesian to spherical polar system:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\  = \ \(sin(\[Theta])\)\ \(cos(\[Phi])\), \
\[IndentingNewLine]y = \ \(sin(\[Theta])\)\ \(sin(\[Phi])\), \
\[IndentingNewLine]z\  = \ \(\(cos(\[Theta])\)\(.\)\)\)]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(predict[t_] := 
      Module[{a, xp, yp, zp, x, y, z, theta, phi, RA, RAm, RAmin, RAsec, DEC, 
          DECm, DECmin, DECsec, s}, \[IndentingNewLine]a\  = \ 
          alpha[t]; \[IndentingNewLine]xp\  = \ 
          Cos[a]; \[IndentingNewLine]yp\  = \ 
          Sin[a]; \[IndentingNewLine]zp\  = \ 
          0.0; \[IndentingNewLine]{x, y, z}\  = \ 
          Transpose[R] . {xp, yp, zp}; \[IndentingNewLine]theta\  = \ 
          ArcCos[z]; \[IndentingNewLine]phi\  = \ 
          ArcTan[x, y]; \[IndentingNewLine]If[phi < 0, \ 
          phi\  += \ 2.0*Pi]; \[IndentingNewLine]RA = \ 
          phi*12.0/Pi; \[IndentingNewLine]RAhr\  = \ 
          Floor[RA]; \[IndentingNewLine]RAm\  = \ \((RA - 
                RAhr)\)*60.0; \[IndentingNewLine]RAmin\  = \ 
          Floor[RAm]; \[IndentingNewLine]RAsec\  = \ \((RAm\  - \ 
                RAmin)\)*60.0; \[IndentingNewLine]Print["\<RA = \>", \ 
          RAhr, \ "\<hr \>", \ RAmin, \ "\<min \>", \ 
          RAsec\ , "\<sec\>"]; \[IndentingNewLine]DEC\  = \ 
          90.0\  - \ theta*180.0/Pi; \ \[IndentingNewLine]If[
          DEC\  \[GreaterEqual] 0, \ s = "\<+\>", s = "\<-\>"; 
          DEC\  = \ \(-DEC\)]; \[IndentingNewLine]DECdeg\  = \ 
          Floor[DEC]; \[IndentingNewLine]DECm\  = \ \((DEC\  - \ 
                DECdeg)\)*60.0; \[IndentingNewLine]DECmin\  = \ 
          Floor[DECm]; \[IndentingNewLine]DECsec\  = \ \((DECm\  - \ 
                DECmin)\)*60.0; \[IndentingNewLine]Print["\<DEC =\>", \ 
          s, "\< \>", \ DECdeg, \ "\<deg \>", \ DECmin, \ "\<min \>", \ 
          DECsec, \ "\<sec\>"];\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(predict[769.875]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[\("RA = "\[InvisibleSpace]12\[InvisibleSpace]"hr "\
\[InvisibleSpace]20\[InvisibleSpace]"min "\[InvisibleSpace]33.46205884748841`\
\[InvisibleSpace]"sec"\),
      SequenceForm[ 
      "RA = ", 12, "hr ", 20, "min ", 33.462058847488407, "sec"],
      Editable->False]], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[\("DEC ="\[InvisibleSpace]"+"\[InvisibleSpace]" "\
\[InvisibleSpace]2\[InvisibleSpace]"deg \
"\[InvisibleSpace]38\[InvisibleSpace]"min \
"\[InvisibleSpace]53.01260973340163`\[InvisibleSpace]"sec"\),
      SequenceForm[ 
      "DEC =", "+", " ", 2, "deg ", 38, "min ", 53.012609733401632, "sec"],
      Editable->False]], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
According to the JPL \"Ephemeris Generator\" (at \
http://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/cgi-bin/eph) the correct answer on 2004-Feb-09 21:00 \
(Local Singapore time) is
RA =  12 hr  05 min  03.94 sec
DEC = + 03 deg  46 min  48.0 sec
So my answer is off by about 15min in RA and about 1 deg off in DEC.  Not bad \
given that we used a very simple model.  Things will not be so lucky for Mars \
but I'll stop here.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Before I really quit,  I just point out that fitting the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x(t), \ y(t), \ and\ \(z(t)\)\)]],
  " to\nx = x0 * cos(t+d1), y = y0 * cos(t+d2), z = z0 * cos(t+d3) \nis \
WRONG!  why, a plot will make this obvious:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(XTdata\  = \ 
        Map[\ {#[\([1]\)], Sin[#[\([3]\)]]*Cos[#[\([2]\)]]} &, \ 
          moondata];\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Short[XTdata, 10]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[\({{0.`, \(-0.44838613370670777`\)}, {0.1666666604578495`, \
\(-0.4709218780561059`\)}, {0.33333333022892475`, \(-0.5017165407468828`\)}, \
{0.5`, \(-0.5452397326994692`\)}, \[LeftSkeleton]4602\[RightSkeleton], \
{767.6666666604578`, \(-0.8631861231219092`\)}, {767.8333333302289`, \
\(-0.883486524569788`\)}, {768.`, \(-0.8964961071721347`\)}}\),
      (Short[ #, 10]&)]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Moon data ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x(t)\)]],
  " vs ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`t\)]],
  " where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\)]],
  " is x-component in old Cartesian coordinate system, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`t\)]],
  " is Julian date offset (starting 1 Jan 2002 00:00 midnight).  Note that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`t\)]],
  " increases by 1 means 1 day has past.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ListPlot[Take[XTdata, 200]]\)], "Input"],

